course: technology
lesson: 40
title: Self-driving cars and machines that run themselves
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A self-driving car is a robot you can sit in. Cameras, radar and lidar, a spinning laser that times how long its light takes to bounce back, build a moving 3D picture of the street, and a computer decides many times a second whether to steer, brake or speed up.
explain: Most cars are not fully self-driving: they have helpers that keep the car in its lane or brake by themselves before a crash, while the driver stays in charge. Some American and Chinese cities already have robotaxis with no driver at all.
explain: Machines that run themselves are all around you: the shop door that slides open as you walk up, the self-checkout that weighs your apples, the vending machine that checks your coin, the cash machine that counts out notes. Each one senses something, follows its program and moves.
explain: This is the last lesson of the course, so look back at the big ideas. People build roads, power stations, cables, satellites and robots; energy is turned from one form to another to make them run; and every machine follows rules people wrote. That is why the safety rules matter: never touch what is dangerous, and always tell an adult.
explain: Machines take over boring, repeating jobs, and people design, build, fix and run them, and new jobs keep appearing. One day the person who invents the next machine could be you.
fact: A self-driving car uses cameras, radar and lidar to scan around it, and a computer decides how to steer, brake and speed up; robotaxis without a driver already carry passengers in some American and Chinese cities.
fact: Even near self-driving cars, always cross the road safely: stop, look and listen.
fact: Lidar is a spinning laser that measures the distance to everything by timing how long its light takes to come back, and it can build a 3D picture of a street.
fact: Driver assistance, like lane keeping and automatic emergency braking, uses cameras to help the driver, and many new cars can brake by themselves to avoid a crash; the driver must still pay attention.
fact: Automatic doors use sensors, some with a radar beam and some with infrared light, to detect people coming, and motors open them; never play in them, because they can close on you.
fact: A self-checkout scanner reads barcodes, and a scale checks the weight of each item, which is why it complains about an unexpected item in the bagging area.
fact: A vending machine checks the money, some by the size, weight and metal of the coin, and then a spiral turns to push the snack forward; never tip or rock one, because it can fall on you.
fact: A cash machine, or ATM, reads the card's chip, checks the PIN with the bank and counts out notes; the first cash machine opened in London in 1967.
fact: A smart home has lights, heating and locks connected to the internet that can be controlled by phone or voice, and a smart thermostat can learn when people are home; the accounts must be kept secure.
fact: Robot dogs are four-legged robots that use motors and balance sensors to walk on rough ground where wheels get stuck, and some inspect places too dangerous for people.
fact: Machines take over repetitive tasks, while people design, build, fix and run them, and new jobs appear as old ones change.
exercise: Which sensors does a self-driving car use to see around it? | Cameras, radar and lidar | Only a microphone on the roof | A thermometer and a clock | A compass and a paper map only
exercise: Where are there already robotaxis with no driver? | In some American and Chinese cities | In every single town in the world by now | Only on Mars, where there are no people about | Nowhere yet, they are only in films and cartoons
exercise: How does lidar measure distance? | It times how long laser light takes to come back | It listens for the echoes of its own engine bouncing back off walls | It counts the lines on the road | It asks the other cars by radio
exercise: What can lidar build of a street? | A 3D picture | A map of the sounds | A printed paper map | A weather forecast
exercise: What is automatic emergency braking? | The car brakes by itself to avoid a crash | The car brakes every minute or so to save fuel on long trips | The driver brakes with both feet | The brakes stop working in an emergency
exercise: With lane keeping and auto braking on, what must the driver do? | Still pay attention | Sleep until the car stops | Play games on a phone | Sit in the back seat
exercise: How do automatic doors know you are coming? | A sensor with a radar beam or infrared light detects you | A guard behind the wall presses a button | They hear your footsteps | They smell people coming
exercise: Why does a self-checkout say unexpected item in the bagging area? | The scale felt a weight that does not match the scanned items | The camera saw you smile | The barcode was the wrong colour | The machine is bored and wants a chat
exercise: How can a vending machine tell coins apart? | By their size, weight and metal | By the date written on each of them | By how shiny and new they look | By the sound you make when you put them in
exercise: Why must you never rock or tip a vending machine? | It is very heavy and can fall on you | It will give you two snacks for the price of one | The snacks inside will all go off | It will ring the shop's alarm and nothing else
exercise: What does a cash machine check before giving money? | The card's chip and the PIN, with the bank | The person's face and height, measured by a camera on the top | The weather outside | The number of coins in your pocket
exercise: Where and when did the first cash machine open? | In London, in 1967 | In New York, in 1973 | In Paris, in 1800 | In Tokyo, in 1999
exercise: What can a smart thermostat learn? | When people are usually at home | How to cook dinner for the family | Which computer games you like best | How to drive the family car to school
exercise: Why give a robot dog legs instead of wheels? | Legs can walk over rough ground where wheels get stuck | Legs are cheaper than wheels and they never ever need any repairs | Legs make it bark louder | Legs help it charge faster
exercise: What happens to jobs when machines take over repetitive tasks? | People design, build, fix and run the machines, and new jobs appear | Nobody works anymore | Machines decide who gets jobs | Only robots get paid
test: «the car drives itself so i can just walk out in front, it'll stop» What is the right rule? | Always cross safely: stop, look and listen | Self-driving cars always stop, so walk out | Walk out only in front of robotaxis | Walk out if you wave at the car
test: «my dad's car brakes by itself so he can play on his phone» What is the best correction? | Driver assistance only helps; the driver must still pay attention | Right, the car does everything now, so the driver can play or even sleep a bit | Only on motorways he can play | He must play only with one hand
test: «let's play in the shop doors, they're like a game, open shut open shut» What is the rule? | Don't play in automatic doors; they can close on you | It is fine if you are fast | It is fine if the shop is empty and nobody needs to come in or out | It is fine if you hold the door
test: «machine ate my coin, rock it till it drops» What should you do? | Never rock it; tell a shop worker or an adult | Rock it gently from the side, just a little bit, until the coin drops out | Climb on it to reach the snack | Tip it forward a little
test: In a cartoon, a cash machine gives money to anyone who smiles at it. What does a real one need? | A card with a chip and the right PIN, checked with the bank | A big smile and a wave | A coin to start it | Only a password said out loud into the little microphone next to the screen
test: What would happen if a self-driving car's cameras and lidar were covered in thick mud? | It could not see the street properly, so it should stop safely | It would drive better with less to look at | It would use its radio to see | Nothing, it drives from memory
test: Count them: a robot dog has 4 legs. How many legs do 3 robot dogs and 1 real dog have together? | 16 | 12 | 8 | 7
test: Count them: at the self-checkout you scan 5 apples, but then put back 2. How many apples should the scale feel? | 3 | 5 | 7 | 2
test: Count them: a vending machine has 4 rows with 6 spirals each. How many spirals? | 24 | 10 | 20 | 28
test: «if robots do all the boring jobs, what's left for me when i grow up, nothing» What is the best answer? | People design, build, fix and run machines, and new jobs keep appearing | Nothing, robots will do every job | Only jobs on Mars will be left | You will have to work for the robots
test: Look back over the course: what do a power station, a phone and a robot vacuum all need to run? | Energy, and parts that follow rules people designed | Only good weather | A person pushing them all day long, taking turns with a friend | Nothing, they run by magic
test: Why is it a good idea that a smart home's accounts are kept secure? | Otherwise a stranger could control its lights, heating or locks | Smart homes break if you use them too much and the heating stops for good | Secure accounts make the heating warmer | The house will forget you otherwise
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of lidar? | Distance is determined by measuring the interval before laser light returns to the instrument | Distance is determined by the echoes of the engine | Distance is determined by counting the markings upon the road | Distance is determined by radio enquiry of other vehicles
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the responsibility of a driver of a vehicle equipped with driver assistance. | The driver shall continue to pay attention to the road at all times | The driver may sleep until the vehicle comes to rest | The driver may occupy a rear seat | The driver may operate a telephone without restriction whilst the vehicle is in motion
exam: Identify the principal cause of the notice concerning an unexpected item in the bagging area of a self-checkout. | The weight detected by the scale does not correspond to the items scanned | The camera has detected the facial expression of the customer and found it displeasing | The barcode is of an incorrect colour | The machine seeks conversation with the customer
exam: Ascertain the consequence, in respect of employment, of the assumption by machines of repetitive tasks. | People continue to design, construct, repair and operate machines, and new occupations arise | All human employment ceases | Machines determine the allocation of employment | Remuneration is paid exclusively to robots
